# Pulsar Architecture Guide

This project uses Pulsar's vertical slice architecture under `app/Pulsar`, with three layers: a Service Layer for HTTP delivery (scoped by consumer audience, e.g., Admin, Client), a Domain Layer for business logic and owned Contracts shared across services, and an Infrastructure Layer for concrete outbound adapters.

`app/Pulsar` is the explicit boundary for Pulsar-governed application code. Files inside it follow Pulsar's placement, dependency, and transaction rules; ordinary Laravel code outside it remains separate from the Pulsar architecture.

A type belongs inside `app/Pulsar` when it calls, is called by, or implements a Pulsar type.
Pure framework bootstrap and configuration that Laravel owns by convention—migrations,
factories, seeders, `bootstrap/app.php`, `config/`, and `routes/`—stay in stock Laravel paths.

## Directory Structure

### Service Layer

Services live in `app/Pulsar/Services` because they are delivery and orchestration boundaries for consumer audiences.

```
app/Pulsar/Services/{Service}/
├── Providers/
│   ├── {Service}ServiceProvider.php
│   └── RouteServiceProvider.php
├── Routes/api.php
└── Modules/{Module}/
    ├── Controllers/    # HTTP handlers (thin)
    ├── Requests/       # Input validation
    ├── UseCases/       # Workflow orchestration
    └── Operations/     # Reusable workflow fragments for UseCases
```

### Domain Layer

Domains live in `app/Pulsar/Domain` because they represent Laravel-aware business capabilities shared across services.

```
app/Pulsar/Domain/{Domain}/
├── Contracts/      # Domain-owned capability ports
├── Models/         # Eloquent models
├── Actions/        # Atomic business operations
├── DTOs/           # Data transfer objects
├── Policies/       # Authorization
├── Events/         # Domain events
├── Enums/          # Business states
├── Exceptions/     # Business rule violations
└── Queries/        # Complex read operations
```

### Infrastructure Layer

Concrete outbound adapters live in capability-oriented areas:

```
app/Pulsar/Infrastructure/{Area}/
└── {Adapter}.php   # Implements a Domain Contract
```

Typical areas are `Payments`, `Search`, `Storage`, `Messaging`, and `Time`.

## Layer Responsibilities

| Layer | Responsibility | What NOT to do |
|-------|---------------|----------------|
| **Controller** | Extract validated data, call UseCase, return response | Never contain business logic; never call Operations directly |
| **Request** | Validate input structure, authorize | No business rule validation |
| **UseCase** | Orchestrate workflows, own transactions, emit events | Never coupled to HTTP Request objects |
| **Action** | Atomic business operation, validate business rules | Don't call other UseCases |
| **Operation** | Reusable workflow fragment across UseCases (conditional branching allowed) | No transactions, no event emission, never called by Controllers |
| **Query** | Complex read operations, reports | Never mutate state |
| **Model** | Data persistence, relationships | Keep thin, no business logic |
| **Policy** | Authorize model abilities with `bool` results and an optional admin `before()` bypass | Default deny; never mutate or start transactions |
| **Event** | Immutable, versioned fact dispatched after commit by default | UseCases emit only; never carry Eloquent models across queued/durable boundaries |
| **Contract** | Domain-owned port for a stable capability | No Services, concrete Infrastructure, helpers, or behavioral calls to workflow types |
| **Adapter** | Implement a Domain Contract and translate vendor/framework errors | No Services, UseCases, Actions, Operations, or other domains' internals |

Generated policies assume the authenticatable is `App\Models\User`. If your application uses a
different user model, update the generated import.

## Code Examples

### Controller (Thin HTTP Handler)

```php
class OrderController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct(
        private PlaceOrderUseCase $placeOrder
    ) {}

    public function store(PlaceOrderRequest $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $order = $this->placeOrder->execute(
            OrderData::from($request->validated())
        );

        return response()->json($order, 201);
    }
}
```

### Request (Validation Only)

```php
class PlaceOrderRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize(): bool
    {
        return $this->user()->can('create', Order::class);
    }

    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [
            'customer_id' => 'required|exists:customers,id',
            'items' => 'required|array|min:1',
            'items.*.product_id' => 'required|exists:products,id',
            'items.*.quantity' => 'required|integer|min:1',
        ];
    }
}
```

### UseCase (Workflow Orchestrator)

```php
class PlaceOrderUseCase
{
    public function __construct(
        private CreateOrderAction $createOrder,
        private UpdateStockAction $updateStock,
        private ReserveInventoryAction $reserveInventory,
    ) {}

    public function execute(OrderData $data): Order
    {
        [$order, $outOfStockProductIds] = DB::transaction(function () use ($data) {
            // 1. Reserve inventory
            $this->reserveInventory->execute($data->items);

            // 2. Create order
            $order = $this->createOrder->execute($data);

            // 3. Decrement stock
            $outOfStockProductIds = [];

            foreach ($data->items as $item) {
                $product = $this->updateStock->execute(
                    $item['product_id'],
                    -$item['quantity']
                );

                if ($product->stock === 0) {
                    $outOfStockProductIds[] = $product->id;
                }
            }

            return [$order, $outOfStockProductIds];
        });

        // Events are immutable facts emitted by the UseCase only.
        // These Event classes implement ShouldDispatchAfterCommit (tier 2).
        event(new OrderPlaced($order->id));

        foreach ($outOfStockProductIds as $productId) {
            event(new ProductOutOfStock($productId));
        }

        return $order;
    }
}
```

### Action (Atomic Business Operation)

```php
class UpdateStockAction
{
    public function execute(Product $product, int $quantity): Product
    {
        // Business rule validation
        if ($product->stock + $quantity < 0) {
            throw new InsufficientStockException($product);
        }

        // State mutation
        $product->update(['stock' => $product->stock + $quantity]);

        return $product->fresh();
    }
}
```

### Operation (Reusable Workflow Fragment)

```php
class SaveAddressOperation
{
    public function __construct(
        private FormatAddressAction $formatAddress,
        private RemoveNullFieldsAction $removeNullFields,
        private ClearDefaultAddressAction $clearDefaultAddress,
        private SaveAddressAction $saveAddress,
    ) {}

    public function execute(array $addressData, User $user): Address
    {
        // 1. Format address (standardize formatting)
        $formatted = $this->formatAddress->execute($addressData);

        // 2. Remove null fields (clean data)
        $cleaned = $this->removeNullFields->execute($formatted);

        // 3. Conditional branching is allowed in Operations
        if (($cleaned['is_default'] ?? false) === true) {
            $this->clearDefaultAddress->execute($user);
        }

        // 4. Save address
        return $this->saveAddress->execute($cleaned, $user);
    }
}
```

Operations are reusable workflow fragments that can be called by multiple UseCases. Sequencing and conditional branching are valid business logic here. They still never manage transactions or emit events.

### DTO (Data Transfer Object)

```php
readonly class OrderData
{
    public function __construct(
        public int $customerId,
        public array $items,
        public string $shippingAddress,
        public ?string $notes = null,
    ) {}

    public static function from(array $data): self
    {
        return new self(
            customerId: $data['customer_id'],
            items: $data['items'],
            shippingAddress: $data['shipping_address'],
            notes: $data['notes'] ?? null,
        );
    }

    public function toArray(): array
    {
        return [
            'customer_id' => $this->customerId,
            'items' => $this->items,
            'shipping_address' => $this->shippingAddress,
            'notes' => $this->notes,
        ];
    }
}
```

### Query (Complex Read Operations)

```php
class GetLowStockProductsQuery
{
    public function execute(int $threshold = 10): Collection
    {
        return Product::query()
            ->where('stock', '<=', $threshold)
            ->where('status', ProductStatus::ACTIVE)
            ->with('category')
            ->orderBy('stock', 'asc')
            ->get();
    }
}
```

## Atomicity: Actions and Queries

**Actions are atomic** — One business operation on one model/aggregate. No calling other Actions or Queries.

**Queries are atomic** — One read operation, return data. No calling other Queries or Actions.

**If you need to compose:** Use Operation (reusable sequence) or UseCase (full workflow).

## Dependency Rules

```
Controllers → UseCases ✅
Controllers → Operations ❌ (always call a UseCase)
Controllers → Actions ❌ (use UseCase instead)

UseCases → Actions ✅
UseCases → Operations ✅
UseCases → Queries ✅
UseCases → Contracts ✅
UseCases → Other UseCases ❌ (extract shared logic to Action)

Actions → Models ✅ (atomic - single operation)
Actions → Contracts ✅
Actions → Queries ❌ (UseCase/Operation passes needed data)
Actions → Other Actions ❌ (compose in UseCase/Operation)

Operations → Actions ✅
Operations → Queries ✅
Operations → Models ✅
Operations → Contracts ✅
Operations → Other Operations ❌
Operations → UseCases ❌
Operations → Events ❌ (emit from UseCase)

Queries → Models ✅
Queries → Other Queries ❌
Queries → Actions ❌

Infrastructure → Domain Contracts ✅
Infrastructure → Services/UseCases/Actions/Operations ❌
Domain/Services → Concrete Infrastructure ❌ (depend on the Contract)
```

### Layer Direction

```
Service Layer → Domain Layer ✅
Infrastructure Layer → Domain Contracts ✅
Domain Layer → Service Layer ❌ (forbidden)
Domain/Service Layer → Concrete Infrastructure ❌
Service A → Service B ❌ (use events for cross-service communication)
```

## Contracts and Infrastructure

Contracts live at `Domain/{Domain}/Contracts/{Name}.php` and use capability names without a
`Contract` or `Interface` suffix: `PaymentGateway`, `Clock`, `FileStorage`. The consumer owns
the port. If Billing needs to take payments, Billing defines `PaymentGateway`; the implementation
lives in `Infrastructure/Payments`.

A Contract's method signatures may use DTOs, Enums, Value Objects, Models, Events, other
Contracts, or Laravel contracts as **passive type dependencies**. A passive dependency appears
in a signature; it is not a workflow call. Contracts never have a **behavioral dependency** on
a UseCase, Action, or Operation, and never import Services or concrete Infrastructure.

Do not turn `Contracts/` into a junk drawer. DTOs, Value Objects, Enums, traits, helper classes,
concrete implementations, framework aliases, and interface-per-class wrappers around pure Domain
logic do not belong there. Create a Contract only for a stable capability with a real alternate
implementation, test fake, or strategy boundary.

An adapter implements the Contract and translates vendor/framework failures into Domain
exceptions:

```php
namespace App\Pulsar\Infrastructure\Payments;

use App\Pulsar\Domain\Billing\Contracts\PaymentGateway;

class StripePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway
{
    // Translate Stripe errors into Billing Domain exceptions here.
}
```

Bind the consumer-owned port in `PulsarServiceProvider::register()`:

```php
$this->app->bind(
    \App\Pulsar\Domain\Billing\Contracts\PaymentGateway::class,
    \App\Pulsar\Infrastructure\Payments\StripePaymentGateway::class,
);
```

Stateless adapters may be singleton-bound. Use `scoped()` for adapters with request- or
tenant-lifetime state, resolve that context through a closure rather than capturing it in the
constructor, and make retryable side effects idempotent.

### Event-name Contracts with per-domain Enums

Use one stable event-name Contract and let each domain own a backed Enum. Do not accept arbitrary
`BackedEnum`, and do not centralize every domain's names in a global god enum.

```php
// Domain/Shared/Contracts/DomainEventName.php
interface DomainEventName
{
    public function eventName(): string;
}

// Domain/Orders/Enums/OrderEventName.php
enum OrderEventName: string implements DomainEventName
{
    case Placed = 'orders.order_placed'; // Persisted/protocol identifier: immutable.
    case Cancelled = 'orders.order_cancelled';

    public function eventName(): string
    {
        return $this->value;
    }

    public function label(): string
    {
        return match ($this) {
            self::Placed => 'Order placed',
            self::Cancelled => 'Order cancelled',
        };
    }
}
```

The backed value is the stable machine identifier. Human labels stay in `label()` and may
change. Event payload schema versions belong on the Event class as `public const int VERSION = 1`.

### Magic-string Policy

| Category | Recommendation | Why |
|----------|----------------|-----|
| Persisted business states | Per-domain string-backed Enum | Closed, domain-owned, type-safe set |
| Persisted event names | Per-domain backed Enum implementing an event-name Contract | Stable machine identifier without a god enum |
| Event categories | Enum | Closed set |
| Authorization abilities | Per-domain string-backed Enum, passed to Gate as `->value` | Domain ownership and no free-floating ability strings |
| Queue connections and queue names | Config keys, referenced by constants | Operational and environment-varying |
| Event listener names | Class names | Laravel discovery and registration are class-based |
| Artisan command signatures | String on the command class | Framework-required, single source |
| Configuration keys | Config files; typed accessor for hot paths | Laravel convention |
| Route names | Namespaced string such as `{service}.*` | Framework string API |
| External protocol identifiers | Immutable constant or backed Enum | Protocol stability |

Closed sets use Enums; open or environment-varying sets use config. Convert Enums to strings at
framework boundaries with `->value`, never reuse human labels as machine identifiers, and never
rename persisted/protocol values casually.

## Dependency Injection (Octane-Safe)

**✅ ALWAYS: Constructor = Dependencies, execute() = Data**

```php
class PlaceOrderUseCase
{
    // Dependencies injected (Laravel resolves once in Octane)
    public function __construct(
        private CreateOrderAction $createOrder,
        private EmailService $emailService,
    ) {}

    // Data passed per request
    public function execute(OrderData $data): Order
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```

**❌ NEVER: Data in Constructor**

```php
// Breaks Laravel Octane - singletons can't hold state
public function __construct(private OrderData $data) {}
```

## Generated Method Conventions

Generated workflow types expose one consistent public method:

| Type | Public workflow method |
|------|------------------------|
| Action | `execute` |
| Operation | `execute` |
| Query | `execute` |
| UseCase | `execute` |

## Transaction Boundaries

**UseCases own transactions** (they see the full workflow):

```php
public function execute(OrderData $data): Order
{
    $order = DB::transaction(function () use ($data) {
        $order = $this->createOrder->execute($data);
        $this->updateInventory->execute($data->items);
        $this->recordPayment->execute($order);

        return $order;
    });

    event(new OrderPlaced($order->id));

    return $order;
}
```

**Actions don't manage transactions** (stay composable):

```php
class CreateOrderAction
{
    // No DB::transaction here
    public function execute(OrderData $data): Order
    {
        return Order::create([...]);
    }
}
```

## Event-Driven Side Effects

Events are immutable facts. They carry IDs, scalars, DTOs, or Value Objects, never Eloquent
models across a queued or durable boundary. Events are created and dispatched by UseCases only;
Actions and Operations never emit them.

Pulsar uses this four-tier rule:

| Tier | Mechanism | Guarantee | Use when |
|------|-----------|-----------|----------|
| 1. Sync in-process | Plain `event()` in the current request | Runs immediately and fires before commit if called inside a transaction | Pure in-memory reactions only; never committed-fact side effects |
| 2. After-commit | Event implements `ShouldDispatchAfterCommit` | Discarded on rollback and dispatched after commit; an in-memory event can still be lost if the process crashes between commit and dispatch | Default for domain events |
| 3. Queued after-commit | Queued listener uses `ShouldQueue` + `ShouldQueueAfterCommit`, or a Job uses `afterCommit()` | Runs on a worker after commit with at-least-once delivery; handlers must be idempotent, and enqueueing is still not atomic with the database write | External calls, expensive side effects, cross-aggregate work |
| 4. Outbox/inbox | Write an outbox row in the same transaction and publish it with a relay | Durable across crashes; consumers use inbox/idempotency for effectively-once effects | Integration events that require guaranteed external delivery |

Tier 2 prevents rollback leaks and read-before-commit races, but it does not close the
crash-between-commit-and-dispatch durability gap. Use tier 4 when that guarantee matters.

The generated Event shape makes tier 2 the default:

```php
use Illuminate\Contracts\Events\ShouldDispatchAfterCommit;

final readonly class OrderPlaced implements ShouldDispatchAfterCommit
{
    public const int VERSION = 1;

    public function __construct(public int $orderId) {}
}
```

**✅ Decouple side effects with events emitted by a UseCase:**

```php
class PlaceOrderUseCase
{
    public function execute(OrderData $data): Order
    {
        $order = DB::transaction(function () use ($data) {
            $order = $this->createOrder->execute($data);
            $this->updateInventory->execute($data->items);
            return $order;
        });

        // The generated Event defers dispatch until commit (tier 2).
        event(new OrderPlaced($order->id));

        return $order;
    }
}
```

**❌ Direct coupling:**

```php
// Don't do this in UseCase
$this->emailService->sendOrderConfirmation($order);
$this->smsService->sendNotification($order);
```

## Service Isolation

Services are delivery slices scoped to a consumer audience (Admin, Client). They share the Domain layer but should not call each other directly.

**✅ Event-based communication from a UseCase:**

```php
class UpdateOrderStatusUseCase
{
    public function execute(int $orderId, OrderStatus $status): void
    {
        $order = DB::transaction(
            fn () => $this->updateStatus->execute($orderId, $status)
        );

        event(new OrderStatusUpdated($order->id));
    }
}

// Client service listens to notify customer
class NotifyCustomerOnStatusChange
{
    public function handle(OrderStatusUpdated $event): void
    {
        // Client-facing notification logic
    }
}
```

**❌ Direct service coupling:**

```php
app(ClientNotificationService::class)->notifyCustomer($order);
```

## Key Patterns

### UseCase vs Operation

| Aspect | UseCase | Operation |
|--------|---------|-----------|
| Purpose | Business workflow (full feature) | Reusable workflow fragment for multiple UseCases |
| Example | `PlaceOrderUseCase` | `SaveAddressOperation` |
| Calls | Actions, Operations, Queries | Actions, Queries, Models |
| Reusability | Specific to one workflow | Shared across multiple UseCases |
| Transactions | Owns boundaries | Never manages |
| Called by | Controllers | UseCases only |

### Action Return Types

Actions can return:
- **Domain objects**: `CreateOrderAction` → `Order`
- **Collections**: `BulkUpdateProductsAction` → `Collection<Product>`
- **Booleans**: `ActivateAccountAction` → `bool`
- **Void**: Operations often return void
- **Primitives**: `CalculateTaxAction` → `float`

### When to Use What

1. **Business logic?** → Action
2. **Workflow orchestration?** → UseCase
3. **HTTP handling?** → Controller
4. **Input validation?** → Request
5. **Reusable workflow fragment shared across UseCases?** → Operation
6. **Complex read?** → Query
7. **Data transfer?** → DTO
8. **Business state?** → Enum
9. **Business rule violation?** → Exception
10. **State change notification?** → Event

## Forbidden Patterns

- Fat Controllers containing business logic
- Controllers calling Operations directly
- Actions calling other Actions or Queries
- Operations calling other Operations or UseCases
- Events emitted by UseCases only; Actions/Operations never emit
- Transactions inside Actions or Operations
- UseCases calling other UseCases
- Request objects leaking past Controllers
- Business logic in Models beyond accessors and scopes

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests (Actions)

```php
test('updates product stock', function () {
    $product = Product::factory()->create(['stock' => 10]);
    $action = new UpdateStockAction();

    $result = $action->execute($product, -3);

    expect($result->stock)->toBe(7);
});
```

### Integration Tests (UseCases)

```php
test('places order successfully', function () {
    Event::fake();
    $useCase = app(PlaceOrderUseCase::class);
    $data = OrderData::from([
        'customer_id' => 1,
        'items' => [['product_id' => 1, 'quantity' => 2]],
        'shipping_address' => '123 Main St',
    ]);

    $order = $useCase->execute($data);

    expect($order)->toBeInstanceOf(Order::class)
        ->and($order->status)->toBe(OrderStatus::PENDING);

    Event::assertDispatched(OrderPlaced::class);
});
```

### Feature Tests (Controllers)

```php
test('creates order via API', function () {
    $user = User::factory()->create();

    $response = $this->actingAs($user)
        ->postJson('/api/orders', [
            'customer_id' => 1,
            'items' => [['product_id' => 1, 'quantity' => 2]],
            'shipping_address' => '123 Main St',
        ]);

    $response->assertCreated()
        ->assertJsonStructure(['id', 'total', 'status']);
});
```

## Commands Reference

| Command | Arguments and options |
|---------|-----------------------|
| `make:service` | `{name}` |
| `make:controller` | `{name} {module} {service} [--resource]` |
| `make:request` | `{name} {module} {service}` |
| `make:use-case` | `{name} {module} {service}` |
| `make:operation` | `{name} {module} {service}` |
| `make:domain` | `{name}` |
| `make:contract` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:model` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:action` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:dto` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:policy` | `{name} {domain} [--model={model}]` |
| `make:event` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:enum` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:exception` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:query` | `{name} {domain}` |
| `make:adapter` | `{name} {area} [--contract={FQCN\|name}] [--domain={domain}]` |
| `publish:context` | `[--force] [--path={path}]` |
| `publish:skill` | `[--force] [--path={path}]` |
| `ping` | — |

